\chapter{Generated scanner and ASDL transport}
\label{ch:71}
\index{Generated scanner and ASDL transport}
\index{LibStone!ASDL}
\index{LibStone!Aurocks}

Aurocks generates the C scanner from the grammar input, and ASDL supplies the token structure and arena support. CMake runs the generators at build time using a selected LibStone checkout. The generated scanner has shared parser state, so the C++ wrapper serializes access. Each invocation owns the source buffer and the C++ copies used after the arena is reset. Neither generator is an application dependency.

\section{Extend both scanner paths}
For a new token, add coverage for exact bytes, offset, display line and byte column. Test adjacency against existing tokens and include the same input in a generated-versus-fallback comparison. Feed a bounded buffer without a trailing NUL to distinguish length-aware scanning from string-style access. Keep token names and the collected locations consistent with the parser's expectations. If generation is unavailable, select the fallback explicitly and record that profile in the verification. The PPX ASDL schema is a separate transport with its own tests.

\section{Recover positions from a backwards list}
Take a seven-byte input consisting of the three bytes \OnxCodeTerm{foo},
a carriage return, a line feed, and the two bytes \OnxCodeTerm{xy}.
Number the bytes from zero. The line-start table begins with zero and
gains the entry five immediately after the line feed. Thus the token at
offset five is on line two, column one; the carriage return occupies
a real byte even though it adds no line-start entry. If the next token
began at offset six, its byte column would be two. This arithmetic does
not depend on the order in which the scanner delivers its tokens.

In the ASDL schema each token points to its predecessor, and the unit
holds the last token. Walking that chain reports the second identifier
before the first. The collector copies text and location as each callback
arrives. The wrapper reverses the collected sequence if it is not already
in increasing offset order; the fallback path supplies forward callbacks.
An unconditional reversal would fix the generated case while breaking
the fallback for precisely this two-token input.

As a diagnostic exercise, replace the final byte by an actual zero byte.
Scanning now fails at line two, column two, before the token stream is
handed to the parser. Compare this to ending the supplied byte span just
before that byte: the shorter input is valid. The pair distinguishes a
length-delimited input from a scanner that silently truncates the source
at the first zero. The size check is independent again: the limit test
accepts a single comment exactly two mebibytes long, but adding one more
comment byte fails before any token has to be recognized.

